# React Component Best Practices and Guidelines

This guide outlines the standardized patterns for creating and maintaining React components in our codebase.

## Component Structure and Organization

### Naming Conventions

- Use PascalCase for component names (`UserProfile`, not `userProfile`)
- Use descriptive, intention-revealing names
- Prefix higher-order components with `with` (e.g., `withAuth`)
- Suffix custom hooks with `use` (e.g., `useFormValidation`)
- Suffix context providers with `Provider` (e.g., `ThemeProvider`)

### File Structure

- One component per file (except for tightly coupled helper components)
- Place related components in a shared directory
- Name files after the main component they export
- Use index files for exporting multiple components from a directory
- Store shadcn/ui components in `src/components/ui` directory

## UI Component Library: shadcn/ui

### shadcn/ui Principles

- shadcn/ui is not a traditional component library but a collection of reusable components
- Components are copied directly into your project, giving you full control of the code
- Follows a "copy and paste" model rather than importing from a package
- Built with Radix UI primitives and styled with Tailwind CSS
- Fully customizable to match your design system

### Adding shadcn/ui Components

- Use the CLI to add components: `npx shadcn-ui@latest add <component-name>`
- Each component is copied directly to your `src/components/ui` directory
- Customize the component code after copying as needed
- Use components that share consistent styling and accessibility patterns

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Using shadcn/ui Button component with variants
import { Button } from "~/components/ui/button";

function MyComponent() {
  return (
    <div>
      <Button variant="default">Default Button</Button>
      <Button variant="destructive">Destructive Button</Button>
      <Button variant="outline">Outline Button</Button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Customizing shadcn/ui Components

- Modify the component source code directly to match your needs
- Customize the Tailwind theme in your configuration file
- Extend or modify the variants in component files
- Create composition patterns with existing components

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Extending shadcn/ui Button with a new variant
// In src/components/ui/button.tsx
const buttonVariants = cva(
  "inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-md text-sm font-medium transition-colors focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-1 focus-visible:ring-ring disabled:pointer-events-none disabled:opacity-50",
  {
    variants: {
      variant: {
        default: "bg-primary text-primary-foreground hover:bg-primary/90",
        destructive:
          "bg-destructive text-destructive-foreground hover:bg-destructive/90",
        outline:
          "border border-input bg-background hover:bg-accent hover:text-accent-foreground",
        // Added custom variant
        custom: "bg-purple-600 text-white hover:bg-purple-700",
      },
      // Other variants...
    },
  }
);
```

### Advanced shadcn/ui Usage

- Combine multiple components to build complex interfaces
- Use Radix UI's composition model for accessible interactive components
- Use the provided hooks and utilities for state management
- Leverage the slot pattern for component polymorphism with `asChild` prop

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Using the asChild pattern with shadcn/ui
import { Button } from "~/components/ui/button";
import { Link } from "~/components/ui/link";

function Navigation() {
  return (
    <Button asChild variant="ghost">
      <Link href="/dashboard">Dashboard</Link>
    </Button>
  );
}
```

## TypeScript Guidelines

### Type Definitions

- Always define types for component props
- Export prop types for reuse and documentation
- Use interfaces for props that will be extended
- Use type aliases for complex union types
- Prefer readonly arrays and properties when applicable
- Use shadcn/ui's exported types when working with their components

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Explicit type definitions
export type ButtonProps = {
  readonly label: string;
  readonly variant?: "primary" | "secondary" | "ghost";
  readonly onClick: (event: React.MouseEvent<HTMLButtonElement>) => void;
  readonly disabled?: boolean;
  readonly children?: React.ReactNode;
};

export function Button({
  label,
  variant = "primary",
  onClick,
  disabled = false,
  children,
}: ButtonProps) {
  // Component implementation
}
```

### shadcn/ui Types

- Import and use the provided type definitions from shadcn/ui components
- Extend shadcn/ui component types for custom components
- Leverage the `VariantProps` utility from `class-variance-authority` for variant props

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Using shadcn/ui types for extension
import { Button, ButtonProps } from "~/components/ui/button";

type CustomButtonProps = ButtonProps & {
  isLoading?: boolean;
};

export function CustomButton({
  isLoading,
  children,
  ...props
}: CustomButtonProps) {
  return (
    <Button {...props}>
      {isLoading ? <Spinner /> : null}
      {children}
    </Button>
  );
}
```

### Export Patterns

- Always use named exports, avoid default exports
- Export types alongside components
- Export constants and utilities that are directly related to the component

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Named exports
export const Button = ({ label, onClick }: ButtonProps) => {
  // Implementation
};

export type ButtonProps = {
  // Type definition
};

// ❌ Bad: Default exports
export default function Button({ label, onClick }) {
  // Implementation
}
```

## Component Props

### Prop Handling

- Destructure props in the function signature
- Define default values in the destructuring assignment
- Use spread props sparingly and only when necessary
- Document complex props with JSDoc comments

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Destructured props with defaults
export function TextField({
  label,
  value,
  onChange,
  disabled = false,
  maxLength = 100,
}: TextFieldProps) {
  // Implementation
}
```

### Props Validation

- Use TypeScript instead of PropTypes for validation
- Be explicit about optional vs required props
- Use union types for props with a finite set of values

## Component Patterns

### Functional Components

- Always use functional components with hooks
- Avoid class components unless absolutely necessary

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Functional component
export function UserAvatar({ user, size }: UserAvatarProps) {
  return (
    <img src={user.avatarUrl} alt={user.name} width={size} height={size} />
  );
}

// ❌ Bad: Class component
export class UserAvatar extends React.Component<UserAvatarProps> {
  render() {
    return (
      <img
        src={this.props.user.avatarUrl}
        alt={this.props.user.name}
        width={this.props.size}
        height={this.props.size}
      />
    );
  }
}
```

### Custom Hooks

- Extract complex logic into custom hooks
- Keep hooks focused on a single responsibility
- Name hooks with the `use` prefix

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Custom hook for form handling
export function useFormField(initialValue: string) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState(initialValue);
  const [touched, setTouched] = useState(false);

  const handleChange = (e: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => {
    setValue(e.target.value);
  };

  const handleBlur = () => {
    setTouched(true);
  };

  return { value, touched, handleChange, handleBlur };
}
```

### Component Composition

- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Use the children prop for flexible component APIs
- Create specialized components for different variants instead of complex if-else logic
- Use shadcn/ui's composable components as building blocks

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Composition with children and shadcn/ui components
import { Card, CardContent, CardHeader, CardTitle } from "~/components/ui/card";

export function UserCard({ user, actions }: UserCardProps) {
  return (
    <Card>
      <CardHeader>
        <CardTitle>{user.name}</CardTitle>
      </CardHeader>
      <CardContent>
        <UserDetails user={user} />
        {actions}
      </CardContent>
    </Card>
  );
}

// Usage
<UserCard
  user={user}
  actions={<Button variant="outline">View Profile</Button>}
/>;
```

## State Management

### Local State

- Use the useState hook for simple local state
- Use the useReducer hook for complex state logic
- Keep state as close as possible to where it's used

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Simple state with useState
export function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Global State

- Use Context API for state shared between many components
- Keep context providers high in the component tree
- Split complex contexts into smaller, focused contexts

## Performance Optimization

### Memoization

- Use React.memo for expensive component renders
- Use useMemo for expensive calculations
- Use useCallback for callback functions passed to child components

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Memoized component
export const ExpensiveComponent = React.memo(function ExpensiveComponent({
  data,
}: Props) {
  // Complex rendering logic
});

// ✅ Good: Memoized calculation
export function DataAnalytics({ rawData }: Props) {
  const processedData = useMemo(() => {
    return expensiveProcessing(rawData);
  }, [rawData]);

  return <Chart data={processedData} />;
}
```

### Lazy Loading

- Use React.lazy and Suspense for code splitting
- Lazy load large components or routes that aren't immediately needed

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Lazy loading components
const LazyDetailView = React.lazy(() => import("../components/DetailView"));

export function Main() {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<Spinner />}>
      <LazyDetailView />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
```

## Error Handling

### Error Boundaries

- Use error boundaries to catch and handle errors
- Create specialized error UIs for different parts of the application

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Error boundary pattern
export function UserProfile({ userId }: Props) {
