## Accessibility

### WCAG Compliance

- Strive for WCAG 2.1 AA compliance at minimum
- Use automated testing tools like axe or Lighthouse to identify issues
- Test with screen readers and keyboard navigation regularly

### Semantic HTML

- Use semantic HTML elements whenever possible
- Prefer semantic elements over generic divs and spans
- Use appropriate heading hierarchy (h1, h2, etc.)
- Use React Fragments to avoid unnecessary divs that break HTML semantics

```tsx
// ❌ Bad: Non-semantic HTML
export function Article({ title, content }) {
  return (
    <div className="article">
      <div className="article-title">{title}</div>
      <div className="article-content">{content}</div>
    </div>
  );
}

// ✅ Good: Semantic HTML
export function Article({ title, content }) {
  return (
    <article>
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      <section>{content}</section>
    </article>
  );
}
```

### ARIA Attributes

- Use ARIA attributes only when necessary (semantic HTML is preferred)
- Always use aria-label for elements without visible text
- Set aria-hidden="true" on decorative elements
- Maintain proper ARIA landmark roles
- Use aria-live for dynamic content updates

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Appropriate ARIA usage
export function IconButton({ icon, onClick, label }) {
  return (
    <button onClick={onClick} aria-label={label}>
      <span aria-hidden="true">{icon}</span>
    </button>
  );
}
```

### Keyboard Navigation

- Ensure all interactive elements are keyboard accessible
- Maintain logical tab order through tabindex
- Implement keyboard shortcuts for common actions
- Handle focus management for modals and dialogs
- Use visually apparent focus indicators

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Focus management for modals
export function Modal({ isOpen, onClose, title, children }) {
  const modalRef = useRef(null);

  // Focus the modal when it opens
  useEffect(() => {
    if (isOpen && modalRef.current) {
      modalRef.current.focus();
    }

    // Return focus when modal closes
    return () => {
      if (isOpen && document.activeElement !== document.body) {
        document.activeElement.blur();
      }
    };
  }, [isOpen]);

  if (!isOpen) return null;

  return (
    <div
      ref={modalRef}
      role="dialog"
      aria-modal="true"
      aria-labelledby="modal-title"
      tabIndex={-1}
      className="modal"
    >
      <h2 id="modal-title">{title}</h2>
      <div>{children}</div>
      <button onClick={onClose}>Close</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Forms Accessibility

- Associate labels with form inputs using htmlFor and id
- Group related form controls with fieldset and legend
- Provide clear error messages with aria-invalid and aria-describedby
- Use input types appropriate to the data (email, tel, etc.)
- Implement validation feedback accessibly

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Accessible form controls
export function AccessibleForm() {
  const [email, setEmail] = useState("");
  const [error, setError] = useState("");

  const validateEmail = (value) => {
    if (!value.includes("@")) {
      setError("Please enter a valid email address");
      return false;
    }
    setError("");
    return true;
  };

  const handleSubmit = (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    if (validateEmail(email)) {
      // Submit form
    }
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <div>
        <label htmlFor="email-input">Email Address</label>
        <input
          id="email-input"
          type="email"
          value={email}
          onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)}
          onBlur={() => validateEmail(email)}
          aria-invalid={!!error}
          aria-describedby={error ? "email-error" : undefined}
        />
        {error && (
          <div id="email-error" role="alert">
            {error}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
      <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

### Color and Visual Accessibility

- Ensure sufficient color contrast (4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text)
- Don't rely solely on color to convey information
- Support reduced motion preferences
- Implement responsive design for zoom and text scaling
- Test with various color vision deficiencies

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Respecting user motion preferences
const slideIn = {
  hidden: { opacity: 0, x: -100 },
  visible: { opacity: 1, x: 0 },
};

export function AnimatedElement({ children }) {
  const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia(
    "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
  ).matches;

  const animation = prefersReducedMotion
    ? { hidden: { opacity: 0 }, visible: { opacity: 1 } }
    : slideIn;

  return (
    <motion.div initial="hidden" animate="visible" variants={animation}>
      {children}
    </motion.div>
  );
}
```

## Performance Optimization

### Component Rendering

- Use React.memo for expensive components to prevent unnecessary re-renders
- Use useMemo for expensive calculations to avoid recomputation
- Use useCallback for event handlers passed to child components
- Avoid inline function definitions in JSX when they cause re-renders

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Memoized component
export const ExpensiveComponent = React.memo(function ExpensiveComponent({
  data,
}: Props) {
  // Complex rendering logic
});

// ✅ Good: Memoized calculation
export function DataAnalytics({ rawData }: Props) {
  const processedData = useMemo(() => {
    return expensiveProcessing(rawData);
  }, [rawData]);

  return <Chart data={processedData} />;
}
```

### State Management Performance

- Keep state as close as possible to where it's used
- Split complex state into smaller, focused pieces
- Avoid unnecessarily deep component trees that cause re-render cascades
- Consider state management solutions (e.g., Zustand, Jotai) for performance-critical applications

```tsx
// ❌ Bad: Passing state through many levels
<App>
  <Header>
    <Navigation>
      <UserMenu user={user} />
    </Navigation>
  </Header>
  <Content>
    <UserSettings user={user} />
  </Content>
</App>;

// ✅ Good: Using context for deeply shared state
const UserContext = createContext(null);

function App() {
  const user = useUser();

  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={user}>
      <Header />
      <Content />
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}

function UserMenu() {
  const user = useContext(UserContext);
  // Render user menu
}
```

### Virtualization for Long Lists

- Use virtualization libraries (e.g., react-window, react-virtualized) for large lists
- Only render items currently in the viewport
- Implement infinite scrolling instead of loading all items at once
- Use pagination for large datasets

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Virtualized list rendering
import { FixedSizeList } from "react-window";

export function VirtualizedList({ items }) {
  const Row = ({ index, style }) => (
    <div style={style}>
      <ListItem item={items[index]} />
    </div>
  );

  return (
    <FixedSizeList
      height={500}
      width="100%"
      itemCount={items.length}
      itemSize={50}
    >
      {Row}
    </FixedSizeList>
  );
}
```

### Lazy Loading and Code Splitting

- Use React.lazy and Suspense for code splitting
- Lazy load components not needed for initial render
- Use dynamic imports for large libraries or rarely used features
- Consider route-based code splitting for multi-page applications

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Lazy loading components
const LazyDetailView = React.lazy(() => import("../components/DetailView"));

export function Main() {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<Spinner />}>
      <LazyDetailView />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
```

### Image and Asset Optimization

- Use responsive images with srcset and sizes
- Implement lazy loading for below-the-fold images
- Optimize image formats (WebP, AVIF) and sizes
- Consider using the next/image component with Next.js

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Responsive and lazy loaded image
export function OptimizedImage({ src, alt }) {
  return (
    <img
      src={src}
      alt={alt}
      loading="lazy"
      srcSet={`${src}-small.webp 480w, ${src}-medium.webp 800w, ${src}-large.webp 1200w`}
      sizes="(max-width: 600px) 480px, (max-width: 1000px) 800px, 1200px"
      width="800"
      height="600"
    />
  );
}
```

### Bundle Size Optimization

- Use import cost to monitor bundle size
- Implement tree-shaking by using ES modules
- Be careful with large dependencies and consider alternatives
- Use bundle analyzer to identify large dependencies
- Remove unused code and dependencies regularly

### Network Performance

- Implement proper data fetching strategies (SWR, React Query)
- Use caching for frequently accessed data
- Batch network requests where possible
- Optimize API responses to include only needed data
- Implement pagination and infinite scroll for large datasets

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Efficient data fetching with SWR
import useSWR from "swr";

export function UserProfile({ userId }) {
  const { data, error, isLoading } = useSWR(`/api/users/${userId}`, fetcher);

  if (isLoading) return <Spinner />;
  if (error) return <Error message="Failed to load user" />;

  return <ProfileCard user={data} />;
}
```

